> just hasn't happened to come up yet?

You always just know the latter; review the concepts of statistical
confidence and sample size. If you deconstruct things far enough,
you'll see that's the only sense in which we really "know" anything.

Anyway: Posit the existence within the game of a way to execute
arbitrary commands (in the underlying OS) as the user launching the
game. (In vanilla NH on UNIX with default build options, the '!'
command will shell out, giving you precisely this.) Given such a
method, it is trivial to demonstrate that you can -- entirely within
the game, mind you -- do things like read news, browse web pages and
RTFS.

If that doesn't fit your definition of "within the game", then
consider things like scrolls of mail, or bones levels containing
engravings left by known-honest perfectly-spoiled players.

